implementationPlan.description:
PROBE THE DISAGREEMENT CASES FIRST, as the routing note instructs — before naming symbols in the plan you implement. Drive the committed registry/public host and the stored-dispatch reader with DELIBERATELY DISAGREEING caller-versus-stored values and record what each answers:
- a caller-presented grant, runtime closure, slot, budget, lease, attempt or process registration that differs from the durable record;
- a replay whose command bytes differ from the stored decision's;
- a node selection naming a node the durable graph does not carry.
Record the exact code and layer each produces, from the landed vocabularies: `FOUNDATION_ATTEMPT_CODES` is now FIFTEEN members after task-6cbff010's produce-or-drop round (RESULT_MANIFEST_INVALID was DROPPED because `buildResultManifest` answers that under the runner's own code at `RUNNER_WORKSPACE_LAYER`, and the contract comment says a second vocabulary for one condition "would only make the two indistinguishable again" — do not re-add it). `FOUNDATION_ATTEMPT_REPLAY_MISMATCH` and `FOUNDATION_ATTEMPT_BINDING_MISMATCH` are the caller-versus-stored codes; `FOUNDATION_ATTEMPT_DISPATCH_IN_PROGRESS` and `FOUNDATION_ATTEMPT_DISPATCH_SUSPECT` cover the replay states.
THE POINT OF PROBING FIRST: the dispatch service already refuses most caller-supplied authority — `durableActivation` reads the activation back "FROM THE STORE, never from the caller's copy and never from the command result the caller received", and `FoundationAttemptDeps` deliberately excludes the runtime filesystem, host observer and clock so "no caller — test or transport — can hand pinning a runtime nobody observed". Ingress must NOT re-validate what dispatch already refuses; a second validator answering first would change which layer refuses and silently detach every downstream assertion. Record which layer answers each case, and design the ingress to add only what dispatch cannot see: authentication, capability, payload allow-list, and node selection.

amendments.description:
TDD RED — DERIVATION OF DURABLE FACTS. Context (re-measured at HEAD e8d1a0a, supersedes steps 1-2's effect.activate route): `foundation.dispatch` IS a RuntimeCommandKind and is registered async in daemon-command-registry.ts:140 with capability WORK and FOUNDATION_DISPATCH_PAYLOAD_KEYS = [activationRequestBytesBase64, binding, graphSnapshot, inputManifest, launchTemplate] (daemon-foundation-command.ts:33). The gap: createFoundationDispatchHandler passes the CALLER's graphSnapshot/inputManifest verbatim to service.dispatch — DoD 2 forbids caller graph facts. Durable readers now exist: `readCurrentActiveGraph(store, projectId)` (apps/daemon/src/planning/active-graph-projection.ts:193, task-dd4ffa0c) and `hydrateFoundationInputManifest` (apps/daemon/src/work/foundation-input-hydrator.ts:200, task-a3e8a02d — its DoD names THIS task as the production consumer). Human ruling 2026-08-19: derive graphSnapshot+inputManifest server-side NOW; launchTemplate stays caller-proposed-but-bounded (codec exact-keys) until task-9a1eb61d lands the server producer.
Write RED tests in NEW file apps/daemon/src/work/foundation-dispatch-derivation.test.ts against a not-yet-existing `deriveFoundationDispatchFacts` (real SqliteEventStore, real git/path observers over a real temp worktree — never a helper that reimplements either; rmSync teardown with maxRetries/retryDelay):
(1) seeded ACTIVE graph revision → derived snapshot's nodes match the durable graph body, with revision identity/graphEpoch provenance;
(2) no ACTIVE revision → exact ACTIVE_GRAPH_ABSENT with the projection's OWN layer, unrestamped; multiple ACTIVE → ACTIVE_GRAPH_SPLIT_BRAIN;
(3) manifest sealed from SERVER-observed HEAD: sealed baseIdentity equals the fixture repo's real `git rev-parse HEAD`, entries are the observed scope's canonical entries; hydrator refusals (e.g. FOUNDATION_INPUT_WORKTREE_MISSING) pass through with their own code AND layer;
(4) cross-project isolation: graph seeded for project A is NOT returned when deriving for project B (exact absent-code).
Plus seam-level RED in apps/daemon/src/http/foundation-registry-hostile.test.ts: a payload still carrying a `graphSnapshot` or `inputManifest` key is REFUSED by the narrowed allow-list — pin the seam's exact unknown-key refusal code and refusing layer (measure which layer answers; refusal, never silent overwrite). Assert positive generated case counts for every sweep (epic rail 6). Run focused; every new test RED for the right reason (symbol absent / key still allowed), recorded per test.

CRASH-RECOVERY E2E + RESIDUE/CONTROL COVERAGE (the v0.2 acceptance clause pinned in this task's comments by worker-bb4011b8, human-confirmed in-row 2026-08-19). Create tests/e2e/foundation/dispatch-crash-sweep.e2e.test.ts reusing the existing live-daemon harnesses (tests/e2e/foundation/e2e-harness.ts, j3-crash-harness.ts patterns, orphan-reap.ts):
(1) drive ONE client-originated `foundation.dispatch` over the ASYNC command entry against a LIVE daemon process — the step-4 derivation makes the client payload originable outside fixtures (only activationRequestBytesBase64 + binding + launchTemplate cross the wire); seed the required durable activation through production commands the way the J-lane harnesses do;
(2) kill the daemon mid-flight BEFORE the recorded event lands (j3-crash-harness owns this timing pattern);
(3) restart and assert `readInFlightFoundationAttempts` (apps/daemon/src/work/in-flight-attempts.ts) returns a NON-EMPTY attempts array on the restarted daemon, and the boot reconciliation sweep (apps/daemon/src/recovery/boot-reconciliation.ts, surfaced through daemon-entry) reports `classified` GREATER THAN ZERO from that same run — pin the exact result shape, not just truthiness;
(4) assert ZERO orphan processes after teardown (orphan-reap pattern) and use retrying rmSync teardown.
ALSO in the focused suites (work/http, not e2e): ZERO RESIDUE — after every refusal arm of the derivation path, read the store back and assert no partial claim/activation/attempt/registration was written (a returned refusal is not evidence nothing was written); and ONE ACCEPTED CONTROL — a fully valid narrowed-payload request that reaches dispatch and returns FOUNDATION_ATTEMPT_RECORDED, so the suite cannot pass by refusing everything. Windows note: run e2e legs from PowerShell, never Git Bash (MSYS path mangling), and budget the reap wait in polls, not wall-clock (the pattern task-97554aa4 landed at e8d1a0a).

MUTATION DRILLS + ADVERSARIAL SELF-REVIEW. Four drills on THIS task's owned production surface (foundation-dispatch-derivation.ts, daemon-foundation-command.ts) — run each alone, record the exact replaced range, restore byte-exact (`git checkout -- <file>` then `git diff` EMPTY, not just status; a peer hook can commit a drill left on disk), confirm each red lands on the INTENDED assertion:
(1) re-add "graphSnapshot" to FOUNDATION_DISPATCH_PAYLOAD_KEYS and pass the caller's value through to the service — the step-3 allow-list refusal test AND the derived-not-caller test must redden;
(2) on a graph-projection refusal, fall back to a caller/staler snapshot instead of refusing — the ACTIVE_GRAPH_ABSENT passthrough test must redden with zero dispatch invocations;
(3) restamp one hydrator refusal into a generic ingress code — the layer-pinning assertion must redden on the lost layer (epic rail 6);
(4) derive with a hardcoded projectId instead of the registry-composed one — the cross-project isolation test must redden.
If any drill stays GREEN the assertion is vacuous: strengthen it before proceeding and say so in the step note either way.
ADVERSARIAL CHECKLIST: (a) producers untouched — foundation-attempt-{service,store,codec,contracts}.ts, foundation-input-hydrator.ts, active-graph-projection.ts, foundation-capture-lifecycle.ts, in-flight-attempts.ts, boot-reconciliation.ts; (b) every new/edited production file <250 lines by `grep -c ''`, split before 400; (c) .js bridges one-line LF, byte-compared; (d) replay assertions still compare against the DURABLE decision, not caller bytes (echo trap); (e) zero-residue read-backs present on every refusal arm; (f) exactly one accepted control exists and asserts FOUNDATION_ATTEMPT_RECORDED; (g) concurrent double-dispatch: same command twice in flight cannot double-launch (service reservation owns this — assert, don't reimplement); (h) `git status --porcelain` shows only owned paths, all drills reverted.
